539568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539925 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0d865ca2-a464-54ee-8571-e4cc8b40fa53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540005 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e64d04fb-cb14-517f-be04-f2bf97158deb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540016 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5a73d02-83b1-5576-b29f-c152341639c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1071 "A lottery decides that $3$ out of every $20$ lottery tickets will win a special prize. If a player wants to win a special prize, what is the probability of finding a prize before purchasing their $5$th ticket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540073 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of tickets purchased until a special prize is found. On average, the chance of finding a special prize on a single ticket is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of tickets that the player should purchase before they find a special prize can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a special prize before the $5$th ticket is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{4-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}+(0.85)^{3}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.478\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1071 "An experiment consists of tossing a fair coin until it comes up heads. What is the probability that we get our first head on the $3$rd toss? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540080 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the coin is tossed until it comes up heads. Since the chance of getting a head in every toss is $50%,$ the number of tosses until it comes up heads can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=50%=\\frac{50}{100}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that head appears in the $3$rd toss is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}(0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1071 "A professional soccer team participates in a championship. On average, the team has a $20%$ chance of losing each game. What is the probability that the team plays at least $4$ games without losing (i.e., the first loss occurs after the $4$th game)?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540087 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of games played until they lose their first game. Since the chance of losing each game is $20%,$ the number of games played before the team loses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=20%=\\frac{20}{100}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.2).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.2)^{x-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{x-1}(0.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>4)=1-P(X\\le 4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{1-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{2-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{4-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.2)+(0.8)(0.2)+(0.8)^{2}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3}(0.2) \\\\  & =[1+0.8+(0.8)^{2}+(0.8)^{3}](0.2) \\\\  & =0.5904.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team will play at least $4$ games before their first loss\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.5904 \\\\  & =0.4096.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1071 "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540104 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11a70fe3-9b0a-5634-8d0e-34e26561e654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540142 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540142 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5dbd1cdf-7537-5f2a-b30a-6dc52b03a1a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540142 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186540073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "689fdd30-f221-5416-a743-4ed6517268b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540155 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540155 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8eb6ad6a-8dea-571b-b04b-4da7ec78d1ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540155 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 175921865400